    11	## 0. Verdict (plain language)
    12	
    13	**The worst case of D′ now rests on coverage alone. Coverage itself is still out of reach.**
    14	
    15	- **Obstacles (b) and (c) of D′ §6 are removed (Theorem R, proved).**
    16	  - (b) was small ord_q(2); (c) was all-FULL pairs.
    17	  - DCL-wc is a modified D′. It is deterministic, costs N^{1/6+o(1)} plus the design's list
    18	    cost, and factors **every** balanced N = pq whose pair is covered by at least one block.
    19	  - It needs no clean band, no order hypothesis modulo the hidden q, and no FULL exclusion.
    20	  - Three standard ingredients do the work:
    21	    - a base-selection dichotomy, a one-sided variant of Harvey–Hittmeir 2026;
    22	    - a γ-order pre-step on the baby list;
    23	    - the exploration of every two-sided collision down the baby product tree. An exact
    24	      collision there reveals Bp + Aq, hence p.
    25	  - The orchestrator asked whether small ord_q(2) ≤ T is exploitable. It is: BSGS costs
    26	    Õ(T^{1/2}) per base and factors N unless ord_p(b) = ord_q(b). That equal-order case is
    27	    handled by other bases and the smooth-subgroup argument (§3, Lemma O).
    28	- **Consequences.**
    29	  - **Theorem W, unconditional cost criterion.** If coverage of every pair can be proved
    30	    with H ≤ √Y·J^{−c}, the deterministic worst-case exponent is
    31	    min(1/5, (1−c)/(7/2−c)). This is below 1/5 iff c > 3/8.
    32	  - Minkowski gives c = 0, so this framework reproduces Harvey's 1/5 worst case (not new).
    33	  - The pair form of DCH (a polylogarithmic H suffices for every pair) now implies a
    34	    deterministic worst-case N^{1/6+o(1)} for balanced semiprimes.
    35	  - Theorem D′'s exceptional set shrinks to the uncovered pairs (its E_b).
    36	- **The worst-case coverage gain is not proved (direction B: no).** I found no design that
