    71	  - *Positive* weights form D′'s V (A ≥ 1, A ≠ B).
    72	  - *Signed* weights also allow A < 0, and we allow the diagonal (1,1).
    73	  - The height is max(|A|, B).
    74	- A **block** is a set 𝒫 of primes ℓ ≡ 3 (mod 4) with H < ℓ < √(N/2).
    75	- The pair (p,q) is **covered** by (𝒫, V) iff ℓ | Bp − Aq for some (A,B) ∈ V and ℓ ∈ 𝒫.
    76	- For ℓ ∤ pq put t_ℓ = p q^{−1} mod ℓ. Then ℓ | Bp − Aq ⇔ A B^{−1} ≡ t_ℓ. So coverage
    77	  depends only on (t_ℓ)_ℓ.
    78	- μ_ℓ(t) is the least height of a weight with A B^{−1} ≡ t (mod ℓ), the first minimum of
    79	  the lattice Λ_ℓ = Z(p,q) + ℓZ² over the weight set.
    80	- h_L(p,q) = min_{ℓ∈L} μ_ℓ(t_ℓ).
    81	
    82	## 2. Theorem W (cost criterion) — proved
    83	
    84	Let J ≤ Y moduli in (Y, 2Y] cover every pair with weights of height H = √Y·J^{−c}. The DCL
    85	list cost is X = HP/Y² + JH², up to N^{o(1)}. Theorem R below supplies the extraction
    86	unconditionally. With J = Y^j, j ∈ [0, 1], balancing the two terms gives the exponent of N
    87	
    88	  E(j, c) = (1 + j(1−2c)) / (5 + 2j(1−c)).
    89	
    90	∂E/∂j has the sign of (1−2c)·5 − 2(1−c) = 3 − 8c. So the optimum is
    91	
    92	  min(1/5, (1−c)/(7/2−c)),
    93	
    94	which is below 1/5 iff c > 3/8. Values: c = 1/4 and c = 3/8 give 1/5; c = 2/5 gives 6/31;
    95	c = 1/2 gives 1/6. (`theory.worst_case_exponent` and the brute-force check in
    96	`test_cost_exponent_formula`; GPT-6.1-sol dchworst_1 recomputed this independently.)
    97	
    98	For J = Y, the target c > 3/8 means: for every pair, some primitive (A, B) with
    99	|A|, B ≤ Y^{1/8−δ} has Bp − Aq divisible by a prime in (Y, 2Y], where P ≍ Y^{25/8}.
   100	
   101	## 3. Theorem R (worst-case extraction from coverage) — proved
   102	
   103	**Algorithm DCL-wc** (`src/dchworst/wcalg.py`; operational: it sees only N and the public
   104	parameters H, V, blocks 𝒫_1..𝒫_K, T and B0).
   105	
   106	0. If N < 4, output FAIL. If N is even, output 2. If N = s² is a perfect square, output s.
   107	1. **Base selection (Lemma O).** For b = 2, 3, …, B0:
   108	   - check gcd(b, N);
   109	   - compute r_b, the least r ∈ [1, T′] with gcd(b^r − 1, N) > 1, where T′ ≥ T. Use
   110	     baby-step giant-step by polynomial evaluation: f(X) = ∏_{i=1}^{s}(X − b^{−i}) with
   111	     s = ⌊√T⌋ + 1, evaluated at X_j = b^{js}. Then scan the first block j whose value has
   112	     gcd > 1.
   113	   - If that gcd is proper, output it. If no r ≤ T′ exists, **select b** and go to step 2.
   114	     Otherwise (gcd = N) record r_b.
   115	
   116	   If every b ≤ B0 was recorded, put Λ = lcm_b r_b and test gcd(1 + jΛ, N) for
   117	   1 ≤ j ≤ (⌊√N⌋ − 1)/Λ.
   118	2. For each block k, with L = ∏_{ℓ∈𝒫_k} ℓ², u_ℓ = b^{L/ℓ²} and γ = b^L:
   119	   - **Targets.** Build the D′ targets τ = (A, B, ℓ, r, c, g_lo, W), with roots of
   120	     Br² ≡ AN (mod ℓ) and the class lock mod ℓ². This is D′ §2.2 step 2 verbatim. For A < 0
   121	     the window bounds are Lo = B a₀ + ⌊AN/a₀⌋ and Hi = B b₀ + ⌈AN/b₀⌉, since
   122	     x ↦ Bx + AN/x is increasing.
   123	   - Choose the baby length m as in D′.
   124	   - **γ pre-step (Lemma G).** Over the babies γ^i (0 ≤ i < m), find the least r ∈ [1, m)
   125	     with gcd(γ^r − 1, N) > 1, using a product tree of the γ^r − 1 and a binary descent.
   126	     - If that gcd is proper, output it.
   127	     - If it equals N, put o = r and run the **equal-order split**. E = L·o is a multiple of
   128	       ord_N(b) whose factorisation is known: the band primes, and o by trial division. For
   129	       each prime power r^e ∥ E, compute y_r = b^{E/r^e} (all at once by the all-but-one
   130	       exponent tree), then gcd(y_r^{r^j} − 1, N) for j = 0..e, and output any proper gcd.
   131	       Otherwise D = ∏ r^{v_r}, with v_r the least j where y_r^{r^j} ≡ 1 (mod N); test
   132	       gcd(1 + jD, N) for 1 ≤ j ≤ (⌊√N⌋ − 1)/D.
   133	   - **Giants.** G_{τ,j} = u_ℓ^{B+AN−c}·γ^{−(g_lo + jm)} for 0 ≤ j < ⌈W_τ/m⌉. Compute
   134	     f(G) = ∏_i(G − γ^i) at all giants by a remainder tree.
   135	     - Output any proper gcd(f(G), N).
   136	     - Collect the **N-giants**, those with gcd = N.
   137	   - **Exploration (Lemma X).** For each N-giant, descend the subproduct tree of f. At a
   138	     node whose value has gcd = N, evaluate both children: output any proper gcd, and
   139	     descend into the children whose gcd is N.
   140	   - **Exact leaf.** When leaf i (the factor X − γ^i) is reached with G ≡ γ^i (mod N), put
   141	     S = c + ℓ²(g_lo + jm + i) and test whether S² − 4ABN = D₀² is a perfect square. If it
   142	     is, output a proper gcd((S ± D₀)/2, N).
   143	3. Output FAIL.
   144	
   145	All outputs are validated (1 < d < N, d | N).
   146	
   147	**Theorem R.** Let N = pq ∈ S_n, and let the parameters satisfy H < ℓ < √(N/2) for every
   148	band prime. Then:
   149	- (i) DCL-wc outputs a proper divisor of N or FAIL;
   150	- (ii) if (p,q) is covered by (𝒫_k, V) for at least one k, DCL-wc outputs a proper
   151	  divisor;
   152	- (iii) its cost is
   153	
   154	  Õ( Σ_k (m_k + T′_k + |𝒫_k| log Y) + B0·T^{1/2} + √N/T + √N/Ψ(√(N/2) − 1, B0) )
   155	
   156	  ring operations mod N, where T′_k = Σ_τ⌈W_τ/m_k⌉ is the giant count.
   157	
   158	  With T = ⌈N^{1/3}⌉ and B0 = ⌈n⁴⌉ the last three terms are N^{1/6}·n^{O(1)}, so the cost
   159	  is N^{1/6+o(1)} + Õ(Σ_k(m_k + T′_k + Y)).
   160	
   161	*Proof.*
   162	
   163	**Lemma O (one-sided order dichotomy).**
   164	- Statement. For b with gcd(b, N) = 1, write o_p = ord_p(b) and o_q = ord_q(b). If
   165	  min(o_p, o_q) ≤ T′, the scan returns r_b = min(o_p, o_q). The gcd at r_b is proper iff
   166	  o_p ≠ o_q. If no r ≤ T′ is found, then o_p, o_q > T′ ≥ T.
   167	- Proof of the dichotomy.
   168	  - b^r ≡ 1 (mod p) iff o_p | r, so the least r with gcd(b^r − 1, N) > 1 is
   169	    min(o_p, o_q).
   170	  - Say r = o_q ≤ o_p. If o_p ≠ o_q then o_p > r, so p ∤ b^r − 1 and the gcd is q.
   171	  - If o_p = o_q, the gcd is N.
   172	  - The BSGS scan visits r = js + i in increasing order inside the first block j with a
   173	    nontrivial value, because b^{js+i} − 1 = b^{i}(b^{js} − b^{−i}).
   174	- Proof of the all-recorded case. Suppose every b ≤ B0 is recorded, so
   175	  ord_p(b) = ord_q(b) = r_b for all b ≤ B0.
   176	  - G_p = ⟨2, …, B0⟩ ≤ F_p^× is cyclic of order lcm_b ord_p(b) = Λ. Likewise mod q.
   177	  - G_p contains every B0-smooth integer in [1, p−1], and these are distinct mod p. So
   178	    Λ ≥ Ψ(p−1, B0), and Λ | p − 1.
   179	  - Hence p = 1 + jΛ with j ≤ (⌊√N⌋ − 1)/Λ (p < √N), and the enumeration finds p.
   180	  - The elementary bound Ψ(x, (log x)^A) ≥ x^{1−1/A}/(log x)^{A−1} follows from counting
   181	    products of k = ⌊log x/(A log log x)⌋ primes ≤ y = (log x)^A: there are
   182	    ≥ (π(y)/k)^k ≥ (log x)^{(A−1)k} of them. With A = 4 and B0 = ⌈n⁴⌉ ≥ (ln p)⁴, this gives
   183	    √N/Λ ≤ P^{1/4}n³.
   184	- The method is the one Harvey–Hittmeir use for large order mod N (arXiv 2601.11131 v2,
   185	  Lemmas 2.1, 2.3 and §3.1, smooth-number subgroup). The one-sided reading "first r
   186	  ⇒ factor unless the two orders are equal" is the only change. ∎
   187	
   188	**Lemma G (γ pre-step).** By the same argument applied to γ over r < m:
   189	- either a proper divisor is output;
   190	- or ord_p(γ) = ord_q(γ) = o < m;
   191	- or **both ord_p(γ) ≥ m and ord_q(γ) ≥ m**.
   192	
   193	In the equal case, γ^o ≡ 1 (mod N) gives o_p(b), o_q(b) | E = L·o.
   194	- For each prime r | E, y_r = b^{E/r^e} has order r^{v_r(o_p)} mod p and r^{v_r(o_q)}
   195	  mod q.
   196	- If v_r(o_p) ≠ v_r(o_q), then at j = min of the two, gcd(y_r^{r^j} − 1, N) is p or q.
   197	- Otherwise o_p = o_q = D, and D is computed. Since b was selected in step 1,
   198	  D = o_p(b) > T. Also D | p − 1, so the enumeration finds p, at cost ≤ √N/T.
   199	- (In the test mode `force_base`, where step 1 is skipped, the enumeration is still complete
   200	  but its cost is not bounded.) ∎
   201	
   202	**The true target.** Let (A, B) ∈ V, ℓ ∈ 𝒫_k with ℓ | Bp − Aq.
   203	- Then a = A N B^{−1} ≡ p² (mod ℓ) is a nonzero square, and p mod ℓ is one of the two
   204	  computed roots r*. This is D′'s coverage criterion; the sign of A plays no role.
   205	- Lemma 1 (class lock): g = (Bp + Aq − c)/ℓ² ∈ Z. Lemma 2 (window): g_lo ≤ g ≤ g_hi, also
   206	  for A < 0 by monotonicity.
   207	- Let j* = ⌊(g − g_lo)/m⌋ and i* = g − g_lo − j*m.
   208	- Mod p: B + AN − (Bp + Aq) = (p−1)(Aq − B), so u_ℓ^{B+AN−c} ≡ u_ℓ^{ℓ²g} = γ^g. Hence
   209	  G* := G_{τ*,j*} ≡ γ^{i*} (mod p) (D′ Lemma 3; no order hypothesis needed).
   210	
   211	**Lemma X (exploration).** Assume both ord_p(γ) ≥ m and ord_q(γ) ≥ m (Lemma G).
   212	- (a) Every giant G has at most one *p-root* i ∈ [0, m) with G ≡ γ^i (mod p), because the
   213	  γ^i are distinct mod p.
   214	  - A node of the subproduct tree with gcd(f_c(G), N) = N contains a p-root.
   215	  - Hence at most one such node per level.
   216	  - The exploration therefore evaluates at most 2⌈log₂ m⌉ node polynomials per N-giant.
   217	- (b) If G* is an N-giant, its exploration outputs a proper divisor.
   218	  - Every node c on the root-to-leaf-i* path satisfies p | f_c(G*), so gcd(f_c(G*), N) ∈
   219	    {p, N}.
   220	  - By induction along the path, either some node yields p (output), or leaf i* is reached
   221	    with G* ≡ γ^{i*} (mod N).
   222	  - In the latter case S = c + ℓ²(g_lo + j*m + i*) = c + ℓ²g = Bp + Aq.
   223	  - Then S² − 4ABN = (Bp − Aq)² is a perfect square, and {(S ± D₀)/2} = {Bp, Aq}.
   224	  - gcd(Bp, N) = p because 1 ≤ B < q, and gcd(|Aq|, N) = q because 1 ≤ |A| < p. ∎
   225	
   226	**Proof of (ii).** Suppose block k covers (p,q).
   227	- Steps 0–1 either output a divisor or select b.
   228	- In block k, Lemma G either outputs a divisor or gives orders ≥ m mod p and q.
   229	- Then G* is computed.
   230	  - If gcd(f(G*), N) = p, it is output.
   231	  - Otherwise gcd = N and Lemma X(b) applies.
   232	- Earlier blocks can only output divisors or continue. Their FAIL branches, the
   233	  enumerations, are complete by Lemmas O and G, so they cannot abort. ∎
   234	
   235	**Proof of (iii).**
   236	- Step 1 costs Õ(B0 T^{1/2}) plus the Λ-enumeration.
   237	- Per block:
   238	  - the exponent tree costs O(|𝒫_k| log Y) mults;
   239	  - targets, giants and the remainder tree cost Õ(m + T′) (D′ §4);
   240	  - Lemma G costs O(m);
   241	  - the equal-order split costs O(|𝒫_k| log Y) plus √N/T.
   242	- Exploration, by Lemma X(a), batched level by level, costs Σ_levels Õ(m + 2#N-giants) =
   243	  Õ(m + T′). Multipoint evaluation of each node polynomial is done at its ≤ 2#N-giants
   244	  assigned points.
   245	- The implementation evaluates the nodes one point at a time, at cost O(m) per N-giant.
   246	  That is fine at test sizes and is charged in `poly_cost`. ∎
   247	
   248	**Corollaries (proved).**
   249	
   250	- **R1 (Minkowski, unconditional worst case N^{1/5+o(1)}).**
   251	  - Design: signed weights with (1,1), H = ⌊√(2Y)⌋, and a block of 1 prime ℓ ∈ (Y, 2Y].
   252	  - If t_ℓ = 1, the weight (1,1) covers. Otherwise pigeonhole on (a, b) ∈ [0, ⌊√ℓ⌋]² gives a
   253	    primitive (A, B) with |A|, B ≤ √ℓ, B ≥ 1 and A ≡ t_ℓB. So every pair is covered.
   254	  - The cost is HP/Y² + O(Y) + N^{1/6+o(1)}, minimised at Y = P^{2/5}: N^{1/5+o(1)}.
   255	  - This is the McKee–Pinch/Harvey exponent, recovered inside the DCL framework. It is not
   256	    new; it is a sanity check that Theorem R is tight at c = 0.
   257	- **R2 (DCH_pair ⇒ worst case 1/6).** Suppose the following, call it DCH_pair(κ). For all
   258	  large n and every (p,q) ∈ S_n, some block of D′'s design with H = ⌈n^κ⌉ covers (p,q),
   259	  with weights from V or with signed weights. Then DCL-wc factors every N ∈ S_n
   260	  deterministically in N^{1/6}·n^{O(κ)} bit operations.
   261	- **R3 (D′ sharpened).** With D′'s own design, DCL-wc fails only on uncovered pairs (D′'s
   262	  E_b).
   263	  - Its exceptional fraction is therefore O(n²/H²), or O(n/H²) with the r16/sieve2d
   264	    denominator refinement, which is not mine.
   265	  - E_a (small ord_q(2)) and E_c (FULL) are gone, and so is D′'s restriction ε ≤ 1/60. Only
   266	    Y > H² (the sieve's distinct classes, ε < 1/7) and H < ℓ remain.
   267	- **R4 = Theorem W made unconditional.** §2 applies with extraction supplied by Theorem R.
   268	
   269	Answer to the orchestrator's cost question:
   270	- BSGS for ord_q(2) ≤ T costs Õ(T^{1/2}) ring ops.
   271	- D′'s E_a threshold is T = N^{1/4−ε}, which costs N^{1/8}.
   272	- It finds a factor unless ord_p(2) = ord_q(2). That case needs Lemma O's other bases and
   273	  the smooth-subgroup argument (≤ n⁴ bases, N^{1/6}n^{O(1)} with T = N^{1/3}). It is not
   274	  automatic.
