   \[
   (p,q)=(2617,5233),\qquad N=13694761,
   \]
   and prescribe its actual ratios at
   \[
   F=\{19,23,31,43,47,59,67\}.
   \]
   The seven ratios are \(16,13,3,17,2,48,58\), respectively. This pair satisfies every prescription, although
   \[
   \prod_{\ell\in F}(\ell-1)=87860868480>2^{24}=P^2>|S_{24}|.
   \]

   Hence both “existence requires the product bound” and “at most six primes” are false. The conclusion that CRT forcing cannot construct uncovered pairs beyond the union-bound regime is consequently **UNVERIFIED**.

   The separate fixed-rational-ratio obstruction is valid: sufficiently many primes dividing one fixed nonzero \(Bp-Aq\) force their product to exceed its absolute value.

10. **MAJOR — B1–B3 need weaker labels; the final “equivalence” is unsupported.**

   **B1 — PROVED conditional calculation.** If the available absolute error budget is \(\gtrsim\sqrt J\), domination requires \(H\gtrsim Y^{1/2}J^{-1/4}\). No argument establishes that every Fourier/counting method must incur that error. “Must beat square-root cancellation” is too broad.

   **B2 — PROVED for the stated single-modulus strategy.** The class-count obstruction and composite-modulus example are sound. They do not prove that every geometry-of-numbers argument is limited to \(c=0\).

   **B3 — PROVED only with its extra coprime-pair premise.** Coverage for every balanced **prime pair** cannot be specialized to \((x,x+1)\). Moreover,
   \[
   \text{single-interval theorem}\Longrightarrow U
   \]
   does not establish a converse. Single-interval records therefore do not give a lower bound on the difficulty of proving \(U\).

   Friedlander–Lagarias Lemma 2.2 supplies an applicable prime-sum estimate. Weingartner’s proof supplies an admissible all-divisor bound yielding \(27/97+\epsilon\) at the relevant parameter. Neither establishes the draft’s claimed necessity or its “best known” status. [Friedlander–Lagarias](https://websites.umich.edu/~lagarias/doc/short.pdf), [Weingartner, Theorem 1 and proof](https://arxiv.org/html/2105.13568v2)

   **UNVERIFIED:** I found no unconditional theorem guaranteeing the requested coverage with fixed \(c>0\) for every balanced prime pair. Umans–Wang Theorem 5.5 remains conditional; He–Sahai refute its AP conjecture variant, not DCH_pair. [Umans–Wang](https://arxiv.org/html/2511.10851v1), [He–Sahai](https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.06681)

   Replace “equivalent” by a sufficient criterion within the stated height/cost parameter family. Also state \(0\le c\le1/2\), or retain the \(H\ge1\) constraint and extraction-cost floor explicitly.

11. **COMPUTATIONAL CHECK — No covered failure found. [PROVED for the runs performed]**

   All six existing worst-case tests passed. Additional scratch tests completed:

   | Runs | Coverage exercised |
   |---:|---|
   | 31,592 | Individual positive, negative, and diagonal covering weights |
   | 1,409 | Full base-selection algorithm |
   | 1,399 | Forced-base runs |
   | 40 | Default \(T=\lceil N^{1/3}\rceil,\ B_0=n^4\), at 39–80 bits |

   These included equal orders for bases \(2,3\); different original orders collapsing to equal \(\gamma\)-orders; band primes shared with \(p-1,q-1\); and exact negative-weight leaves.

   Representative successful branches were \(104653=229\cdot457\) through \(D\)-enumeration, \(181429=397\cdot457\) through valuation splitting, and \(13694761\) through \(\Lambda\)-enumeration.

