   \text{single-interval theorem}\Longrightarrow U
   \]
   does not establish a converse. Single-interval records therefore do not give a lower bound on the difficulty of proving \(U\).

   Friedlander–Lagarias Lemma 2.2 supplies an applicable prime-sum estimate. Weingartner’s proof supplies an admissible all-divisor bound yielding \(27/97+\epsilon\) at the relevant parameter. Neither establishes the draft’s claimed necessity or its “best known” status. [Friedlander–Lagarias](https://websites.umich.edu/~lagarias/doc/short.pdf), [Weingartner, Theorem 1 and proof](https://arxiv.org/html/2105.13568v2)

   **UNVERIFIED:** I found no unconditional theorem guaranteeing the requested coverage with fixed \(c>0\) for every balanced prime pair. Umans–Wang Theorem 5.5 remains conditional; He–Sahai refute its AP conjecture variant, not DCH_pair. [Umans–Wang](https://arxiv.org/html/2511.10851v1), [He–Sahai](https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.06681)

   Replace “equivalent” by a sufficient criterion within the stated height/cost parameter family. Also state \(0\le c\le1/2\), or retain the \(H\ge1\) constraint and extraction-cost floor explicitly.

